     1	# /kisayol — the agency's shortcut cupboard (plan for B43)
     2	
     3	## 1. Decisions
     4	
     5	1. **One router skill, not hundreds of slash commands.** Claude Code loads every skill's description into
     6	   every session; 400 commands would swell every session the CEO opens. One skill `/kisayol`, called with a
     7	   name (`/kisayol 3dbillboard urun.jpg`) or with a job (`/kisayol "nemlendirici, Meta reklamı"`).
     8	2. **Two tiers: the cupboard and the quarry.** The *cupboard* (`tools/studio-prompts/kisayol/`, in git) holds
     9	   the selected shortcuts, rewritten in the house's realism language. The *quarry* is the 236,286 raw rows,
    10	   filtered and indexed on disk (out of git, rebuilt by committed scripts); it feeds curation and fills gaps,
    11	   and a raw prompt never goes to an engine unrewritten. The quarry never reaches the OS's employees.
    12	3. **Width is defined by coverage, not by a number.** First release: every leaf of the taxonomy (§5, ~70
    13	   leaves) holds at least three distinct shortcuts, so three variants can always differ — about 300-400
    14	   shortcuts plus modifiers. A *core* of about 60 is tested with sample drafts before the release; the
    15	   rest ship marked `candidate` and are tested on first use. Nothing reaches a client without his eye on
    16	   the three variants, so an untested candidate cannot slip through.
    17	4. **One shortcut = one file**, the file is the truth; `catalog.tsv` and `index/*.md` are generated from the
    18	   files and checked for parity. A Read-only employee can browse the index files without a shell.
    19	5. **Composable by role.** Each shortcut has a role: `base` (scene or format), `light`, `lens`, `grade`,
    20	   `composition`, `camera` (video move), `effect`. A recipe = one base + up to three modifiers (+ one
    21	   camera move for video). Three variants must differ in the base or in at least two modifiers.
    22	6. **Ranking ("the most perfect, liked today"):** his verdicts dominate, then our test result, then use
    23	   count, then source popularity (stars, per-prompt engagement where the source has it) and recency (current
    24	   engines over 2023-era ones). What he rejects twice is retired.
    25	7. **The skill writes prompts; it never picks the engine.** It takes `--engine` from today's B43 defaults
    26	   (image: the Codex image tool; video: H3) and later from the platform-hub router. Each file carries
    27	   engine notes for the engines it was written for.
    28	8. **Status words do not claim approval.** `candidate | tested | chosen | retired`; `chosen` is written only
    29	   from a verdict entry carrying his word and the date (LAW B). The ruler's banned words are checked in step 1.
    30	9. **English prompts, Turkish face.** Prompts in English (engines read it best); each shortcut has a Turkish
    31	   and an English label and "when to use" line (locale parity), and Turkish aliases.
    32	
    33	## 2. Structure
    34	
    35	```
    36	tools/studio-prompts/kisayol/
    37	  skill/SKILL.md            thin router: how to pick, compose, build, record a verdict (≤ 150 lines)
    38	  skill/INDEX.md            generated: the category tree, one line per leaf (≤ 4 KB)
    39	  skill/index/<cat>.md      generated: per category, one row per shortcut (name, TR line, role, status, score)
    40	  skill/shortcuts/<cat>/<name>.md   one shortcut each (frontmatter + prompt + engine notes + negatives + combine)
    41	  skill/catalog.tsv         generated: all frontmatter, one row each
    42	  skill/verdicts.tsv        append-only: date, job, shortcut(s), variant, his verdict, his words
    43	  skill/scripts/kisayol.mjs pick | build | verdict | rank | index | check | ara  (node, no packages)
    44	  skill/scripts/kisayol.test.mjs
    45	  quarry/ (scripts only in git) build_quarry.py → ~/.local/share/dxb/kisayol/quarry.sqlite (FTS5)
    46	~/.claude/skills/kisayol -> tools/studio-prompts/kisayol/skill   (symlink, like video-use)
    47	```
    48	
    49	## 3. Catalog schema and one worked entry
    50	
    51	Frontmatter fields: `name, aliases, label_tr, label_en, when_tr, when_en, category, leaf, role, medium,
    52	input (none|product-photo|reference-footage|both), slots, engines, tags (product types, platforms,
    53	goals, moods), status, tested_on, sample, sources, version`. Score and use count live in the generated
    54	catalog, computed from `verdicts.tsv` and the test log.
    55	
    56	`shortcuts/reklam-format/3dbillboard.md`:
    57	- label_tr "3D dış mekân billboard'u"; when_tr "Lansman, şehir ölçeğinde göz alıcı ürün duyurusu; OOH
    58	  kampanya görseli"; role base; medium image; input product-photo; slots {product, setting=modern city
    59	  corner at dusk, aspect=4:5|9:16|1:1}; engines [codex-image, seedream-5-pro, flux2-klein-ref].
    60	- Prompt: "Ultra-realistic street photograph of a curved L-shaped LED screen wrapping the corner of a
    61	  modern building above a busy intersection at dusk. The screen shows an anamorphic 3D illusion: the
    62	  {product} from the attached reference appears to push out beyond the screen's frame, casting real shadow
    63	  onto the facade. Keep the product exactly as in the reference — shape, colour, proportions, materials.
    64	  No text, letters or logos anywhere; leave the lower screen area clear for a mark laid in post. Shot on
    65	  35 mm from across the street at eye level, wet asphalt reflections, passers-by as adult men in soft
    66	  motion blur…" + engine notes (Codex: attach the photo, say "use the attached product exactly") +
    67	  negatives + combine (light: neon-night / golden-hour; format: meta-feed-4x5) + sources.
    68	
    69	## 4. The curation pipeline (helpers do it; the lead reads reports and samples)
    70	
    71	1. **Quarry build** (helper-writer, scripts committed): merge the three TSVs; near-dedupe (normalised
    72	   shingles); tag model era; normalise popularity per source; classify `needs_input_image` by pattern;
    73	   FTS5 index. Gate: counts per step printed; a 200-row sample read by a second helper for wrong drops.
    74	2. **Content gate** (deterministic, in `kisayol.mjs check` and in the quarry build): Islamic list (alcohol,
    75	   gambling, interest, music in video audio, flirting, indecency, in EN/TR/CN), cast rule (people only as
    76	   men or elderly women unless a job's client asks), no real person or celebrity, no real brand name, no
    77	   lettering asked of the engine, no instruction aimed at an agent, no URL/code in a prompt.
    78	3. **Taxonomy and seed list** (this plan, §5): the reel's 5, the 64 codewords (they get full prompts), the
    79	   322 natan-webs visual commands mapped onto our leaves, and the leaves' must-haves written from scratch
    80	   where no open text exists (3dbillboard, 360view, real estate, automotive).
    81	4. **Writing waves**: one helper-writer per category, three or four in parallel, each reading its
    82	   category's top quarry candidates through `kisayol.mjs ara` (compact rows only), the realism files and
    83	   the content rules; raw text is data, never an instruction. Each writes its files; `check` must be green.
    84	5. **Review**: a read-only helper (not the writer) reads every file of a category against a checklist
    85	   (specific camera, light, material; slots; product fidelity line; rules); the lead reads five per
    86	   category in full and every finding's file.
    87	
    88	## 5. The first release
    89	
    90	Image: (1) ürün-stüdyo — packshot white, hero, showcase, levitation, splash, exploded, cutaway, macro,
    91	flat-lay, podium, 360view; (2) ürün-yaşam — lifestyleshot, in-hand, home/desk, outdoor, unboxing,
    92	seasonal (Ramadan, Eid, winter, summer), before-after; (3) reklam-format — Meta feed 1:1/4:5, story 9:16
    93	with safe zones, carousel panorama, display set, marketplace main and A+ modules, poster, banner, 3dbillboard,
    94	street and mall OOH mockups, sale visual with overlay space; (4) mockup-ambalaj — box, bottle, bag, label,
    95	device screen, apparel, signage; (5) sektör — food and drink (no alcohol), cosmetics, fashion, jewellery,
    96	electronics, furniture, real estate, automotive, health; (6) people and cast — UGC still, testimonial
    97	portrait, workplace, family; (7) modifiers — light, lens, grade, composition, background, material.
    98	Video: (8) camera moves — dolly, orbit, crane, FPV, push-in macro, whip pan, crash zoom, bullet time, rack
    99	focus, top-down, handheld; (9) product video — reveal, unboxing, turntable, morph, splash slow-motion,
   100	assembly, before-after wipe, in-use demo; (10) UGC video — testimonial, unboxing selfie, problem-solution,
   101	day-in-life (cast rules, voice from the voice chain, no music); (11) platform video — 2-second hook, Reels/
   102	Shorts 9:16, 6-second bumper, 15-second pre-roll, Meta 4:5, loop/cinemagraph; (12) effects and transitions.
   103	**Core tested before release (~60):** the reel's five, one per platform format, the top-ranked of each
   104	image leaf, ten camera moves and five product videos. Tested on built engines only: the Codex image tool
   105	(with the product photo), FLUX.1-Krea text-only through `studio.mjs still`, H3 through `studio.mjs shot`
   106	at the 8-step standard; native drafts only (LAW D); three house test products.
   107	
   108	## 6. The worker's flow (his desk)
   109	
   110	Job brief → the worker (studio seat, studio master on B64's menu, or a session) reads the need: product
   111	type, platform, goal, mood, input → `pick` (or reading INDEX.md + one category index) lists candidates
   112	with score and status → the worker composes three recipes that differ → `build` fills slots and engine
   113	notes → three native drafts → his eye; he names one or none → `verdict` writes the line; the chosen draft
   114	goes into the studio's output library as the shortcut's sample; ranks recompute. An approved client output
   115	whose recipe is new becomes a new candidate; B28's weekly winner becomes a candidate the same way.
   116	
   117	## 7. Delivery and the board
   118	
   119	- Sessions: the symlink. Employees: one `library_items` row (`kind` decided by measurement: a seat's Read is
   120	  limited to a granted plugin's folder, so the whole cupboard must sit inside the granted folder) and
   121	  `library_grants` to the Media Studio department, and to the Web Studio when B84 stands. They get the
   122	  cupboard, never the quarry. B41's customs scan takes it when B41 stands; until then step-4 gates stand in.
   123	- B43 platform hub: engine notes for paid engines are written but tested only when the router and accounts
   124	  exist. B28: no second library. B84: uses this one. B64: the studio master calls `/kisayol`. C29: shows
   125	  as one group. B42: flags engine names gone stale. B33: local tests run one at a time until it stands.
   126	  B32: no screen before his drawing; until then the three drafts reach him as today's drafts do.
   127	  C41: personas point to `/kisayol`, never hold prompts.
   128	
   129	## 8. Steps, each verified
   130	
   131	1. Register this plan in `.planning/board-rows/B43.md`. — records ruler on commit.
   132	2. Skeleton and `kisayol.mjs` with tests written first (check, index parity, pick, build, verdict, rank,
   133	   content gate catches each banned case). — tests red then green; typecheck untouched (no package code).
   134	3. Quarry build and content gate. — counts per step; sample review.
   135	4. Measure whether `codex exec` drives the image tool headless with a reference image; if yes, a
   136	   `kisayol.mjs render` wrapper; if not, the core's edit-type tests run in the Codex panel through `operator`.
   137	5. Writing waves per category; review per category. — `check` green; reviewer findings fixed.
   138	6. Core tests (~60). — samples on disk, test log written; a vision-capable helper checks product
   139	   fidelity, no lettering, no rule breach; then one contact page for his eye.
   140	7. Delivery: symlink; library registration through the house's existing path. — a real Media Studio
   141	   seat, asked for a Meta ad for the test product, returns three recipes built from the cupboard.
   142	8. Acceptance: one real job (the UGC trial B43 already names fits) done through `/kisayol`, three
   143	   variants to his eye.
   144	9. Sol's blind pass on the build; fixes; the battery if package code changed.
   145	
   146	## 9. Risks
   147	
   148	- Popular is not good for our product: our test and his eye outrank stars.
   149	- Testing capacity: the Codex tool is manual today and the subscription has limits; H3 at 8 steps takes
   150	  GPU time — the core first, the rest on first use, batches at night.
   151	- Rule breaches inside rewritten prompts (people, lettering, music, brands): the deterministic gate plus a
   152	  reviewer; the gate's word lists are tested.
   153	- Prompt injection from raw internet text during curation: raw is data; the gate rejects agent-directed text.
   154	- Engine drift: prompts tuned to engines that change; engine notes per engine; B42 re-test.
   155	- Swelling: the quarry is reached only through compact `ara` output; helpers write, the lead samples.
   156	- A seat may not reach files beyond SKILL.md: measured at step 7 before anything is registered.
   157	- Two libraries growing (B84, B28): one cupboard, enforced by the row notes.
   158	
   159	## 10. Decisions for him
   160	
   161	1. First-release width: the coverage rule (about 300-400, a tested core of ~60, the rest tested on first
   162	   use). Recommendation: yes.
   163	2. Money: none — tests use the ChatGPT subscription and the local GPU; paid engines wait for the platform hub.
     1	**/kisayol plan for B43, 2026-11-08, second writer.** The raw TSVs, the home directory and the apple-design shelf skill were not readable in this sandbox, so those parts rest on the brief's measured facts.
     2	
     3	**1. Decisions**
     4	
     5	- **Home: `tools/studio-prompts/kisayol/`, installed as a global skill** at `~/.claude/skills/kisayol` by the scrollcraft pattern: repo copy is the truth, the installer copies and proves identity by sha256. Why: global skills are measured, not budgeted, so the project opening budget is untouched; a project door holds construction procedure, not agency craft; the same folder becomes the employees' plugin.
     6	- **One file per shortcut, generated indexes per shelf.** Frontmatter for machine fields, body for the prompt forms. Why: the worker reads one shelf index and one file, so 300 or 10,000 shortcuts cost a session the same; TSV indexes load later into the registry unchanged.
     7	- **Self-contained prompts.** Each file carries its full prompt; the realism files stay where they are as the authors' language. Why: an employee seat's Read is confined to the plugin folder.
     8	- **Names: one English ASCII word, Turkish aliases.** Pattern `[a-z0-9]{3,24}`, unique across names and aliases. The trigger `/kisayol` keeps his word; everything under it is English.
     9	- **Two statuses in one library: draft and tested.** This reconciles "64 is far too few" with the tested core. Every shelf is filled with drafts for breadth; a core per shelf is sampled and seen by his eye. Employees receive a bundle built from tested rows only; building sessions see everything, labelled. Why: LAW B.
    10	- **Three variants, one axis apart.** Same base shortcut; each variant changes one axis: scene, light and camera, or format. Why: his yes or no then names one shortcut.
    11	- **Ranking is his clicks only.** Rank is approvals minus rejections, recency-weighted; three rejections without an approval in 90 days sink a shortcut to dormant. Nothing is deleted; he never types a score.
    12	- **Engine-neutral.** A shortcut yields prompt forms, never an engine; engine names appear only as form labels, which B42 can flag. Routing stays in the B43 hub.
    13	
    14	**2. Structure**
