     1	# Realism prompts — the source sheet, verbatim
     2	
     3	**Source:** "AI Video Realism & Cinematic Prompt Sheet" by **@heyypriyank** (Instagram reel
     4	https://www.instagram.com/reel/DeT2uHIuZUb/), the single PDF in his public Google Drive folder
     5	`1Uxz5Y2z3VqDxg5I7IeLuiHi9pWKdAYnc` ("Ai Realism", `AI_Video_Realism_Cinematic_Prompt_Sheet.pdf`, 3 pages,
     6	read 2026-11-07). Everything inside the code blocks below is his text, unchanged; the "When" notes are ours.
     7	
     8	The sheet is written for a **cinema-camera** look. It serves any studio job that has to look camera-captured: ads,
     9	product films, brand films, the stills and loops of a ScrollCraft site, and UGC. For the phone-shot creator look use the
    10	UGC version beside this file: `ugc-realism-prompts.md`. The studio's UGC recipe is `.planning/board-rows/B43.md`,
    11	§ "Hyper-realistic UGC — the factory recipe (2026-11-07)".
    12	
    13	**How the modules combine:** start from one subject prompt (01, 03, 06, 10, 11 or 12), then add only the modules the
    14	shot needs: skin (01, 02), hair (07), material (08), reflections (14), grain (04), lens (05), colour (09), one camera
    15	move (12 or 13). Paste the negative list (section 4) into the generator's negative field. Where there is no negative
    16	field, write its most important items into the prompt as instructions (his tip in section 2). In our studio that is
    17	the Codex image tool, FLUX.2-klein, and MiniMax H3 at its default `--cfg 1.0` (`run.py` reads `--negative` only above
    18	1.0).
    19	
    20	## 1. The key principle
    21	
    22	```text
    23	Instead of relying only on words like “8K,” “ultra-realistic,” or “cinematic”, describe observable details: how skin reflects light, how fabric behaves, where the light comes from, how the camera moves, and what small imperfections exist. Add only the details that fit your shot.
    24	```
    25	
    26	## 2. What to write — and what to avoid
    27	
    28	```text
    29	Prefer: natural skin texture, visible pores, fine lines, subtle blemishes, realistic specular highlights, physically plausible shadows, fine film grain, natural motion blur, restrained sharpening, real-world lens behavior, consistent anatomy, believable reflections, and motivated lighting.
    30	
    31	Avoid or limit: airbrushed / waxy skin, plastic-looking surfaces, excessive beauty retouching, oversharpening, oversaturated colors, fake HDR, perfectly uniform textures, glowing edges, warped hands, changing facial features, flickering details, impossible reflections, and excessive camera movement.
    32	
    33	Tip: negative prompts are supported only by some generators. If yours has no negative-prompt field, fold the most important exclusions into the main prompt as instructions.
    34	```
    35	
    36	## 3. 15 copy-ready prompts
    37	
    38	```text
    39	Replace bracketed text with your subject, action, location, light source, or lens choice. Use the full prompt or combine the relevant parts for your generator and shot.
    40	```
    41	
    42	### 01 — Natural skin and visible pores
    43	
    44	**When:** any close-up or medium close-up of a face: beauty, skincare and cosmetics ads, a presenter's hero frame,
    45	portrait stills. Leave pores out of wide shots (workflow step 2).
    46	
    47	```text
    48	Close-up portrait of [SUBJECT], with authentic human skin texture: visible pores, fine vellus hair, subtle fine lines, tiny natural blemishes, and realistic variation in skin tone. Soft directional window light reveals the texture without making it harsh. Natural highlights on the forehead, nose, and cheeks; no beauty-filter finish. Photographed with a real cinema camera, 85mm portrait lens, shallow but believable depth of field, restrained color grading, lifelike detail.
    49	```
    50	
    51	### 02 — Remove the plastic / waxy look
    52	
    53	**When:** a second pass on a generated still or frame whose skin or surfaces came out smooth and glossy. It keeps
    54	identity and pose, so it is safe on an image the client has already seen.
    55	
    56	```text
    57	Make [SUBJECT] look naturally photographed rather than computer-generated. Preserve irregular skin texture, fine hairs, natural facial asymmetry, realistic roughness, and soft transitions between light and shadow. Avoid waxy skin, overly smooth surfaces, synthetic gloss, uniform pores, and excessive sharpening. Keep the original identity, proportions, pose, and scene.
    58	```
    59	
    60	### 03 — Realistic cinematic lighting
    61	
    62	**When:** interiors and product scenes where the light has to come from something visible in the frame: brand films,
    63	premium ads, the lit rooms of a ScrollCraft world.
    64	
    65	```text
    66	A believable cinematic shot of [SUBJECT] lit by a motivated practical source: [WINDOW / LAMP / SUNSET]. Soft falloff across the face, natural shadow density, subtle bounce light, realistic highlights, and no unexplained glow. Preserve detail in both highlights and shadows. Filmic contrast, restrained saturation, natural skin tones, physically plausible light direction.
    67	```
    68	
    69	### 04 — Fine film grain, not digital noise
    70	
    71	**When:** the finish of a cinematic still or clip that looks too clean. In post the studio adds grain with FFmpeg
    72	(B43 step 8); use this in the prompt only when the generator has to carry it.
    73	
    74	```text
    75	Apply very subtle, fine, organic film grain that is consistent across the image and appropriate to the exposure. Keep skin texture and fine details intact. Avoid chunky noise, color speckles, compression artifacts, excessive grain, and artificial texture overlays. A restrained cinematic finish, with clean midtones and natural highlight roll-off.
    76	```
    77	
    78	### 05 — Real camera and lens behavior
    79	
    80	**When:** any shot meant to read as filmed on a professional camera: ads, product films, brand films, website hero
    81	loops. Pick one focal length: 35 mm for rooms and context, 50 mm for people at mid distance, 85 mm for faces.
    82	
    83	```text
    84	Footage that feels captured on a real cinema camera with a [35mm / 50mm / 85mm] lens. Natural depth of field, realistic focus falloff, gentle lens character, subtle optical imperfections, and believable motion blur. Keep geometry stable and avoid extreme bokeh, fake lens flares, distorted perspective, or digitally sharpened edges.
    85	```
    86	
    87	### 06 — Natural movement and facial performance
    88	
    89	**When:** any clip with a person on screen: presenters, testimonials, actors in an ad. It is the module that keeps the
    90	face the same from the first frame to the last.
    91	
    92	```text
    93	A candid, lifelike moment of [SUBJECT] performing [ACTION]. Use subtle human micro-movements, natural blinking, realistic breathing, small shifts in posture, and physically plausible timing. Keep facial identity and anatomy consistent from frame to frame. No frozen expressions, rubbery motion, sudden warping, or exaggerated gestures.
    94	```
    95	
    96	### 07 — Realistic hair and fine details
    97	
    98	**When:** hair in frame, and above all when it moves (wind, a head turn, hair-care ads). Eyebrows and lashes matter in
    99	every face close-up.
   100	
   101	```text
   102	Render hair with individual strands, natural flyaways, varied strand thickness, and believable movement responding to gravity and airflow. Preserve natural eyebrows, eyelashes, and fine facial hair. Avoid painted-on hair, clumped strands, shimmering edges, flicker, and details that change shape between frames.
   103	```
   104	
   105	### 08 — Real fabric and material texture
   106	
   107	**When:** fashion, apparel and accessory work, and any product whose surface is the sale (leather, metal, glass,
   108	packaging). It is the module to use when one frame holds several materials side by side.
   109	
   110	```text
   111	Show [CLOTHING / MATERIAL] with physically believable weave, seams, folds, wrinkles, and light response. Fabric should move with the body and gravity; leather, metal, glass, and skin should each have distinct surface properties. Avoid uniform textures, rubber-like fabric, floating folds, and reflections that do not match the environment.
   112	```
   113	
   114	### 09 — Cinematic color grading without overprocessing
   115	
   116	**When:** whenever the colour is set in the prompt rather than in post: premium ads, brand films, site imagery that
   117	must sit with a brand palette. Final colour still belongs to the editor (workflow step 6).
   118	
   119	```text
   120	Use a restrained cinematic grade with natural skin tones, controlled highlights, rich but realistic shadows, and moderate saturation. Preserve color separation without teal-orange exaggeration. Keep whites neutral where appropriate and retain texture in dark areas. Avoid crushed blacks, neon colors, excessive contrast, fake HDR, and orange skin.
   121	```
   122	
   123	### 10 — Realistic outdoor daylight
   124	
   125	**When:** exteriors in daylight: travel, real estate, outdoor products, street scenes, landscapes behind a product.
     1	# UGC realism prompts — the 15 modules in phone language
     2	
     3	The UGC (phone-shot, creator-style) version of every prompt in `realism-prompts.md`, rewritten by the studio
     4	(2026-11-07) from @heyypriyank's sheet (reel DeT2uHIuZUb; credit and the verbatim originals in that file). The physics
     5	vocabulary for skin, light, hair, fabric and reflections stays; the cinema camera becomes a phone: front camera or the
     6	main camera, arm's-length selfie, auto exposure, slight sensor noise, mixed home light, imperfect framing. The recipe
     7	these forms serve is `.planning/board-rows/B43.md`, § "Hyper-realistic UGC — the factory recipe (2026-11-07)".
     8	
     9	## How to use the forms
    10	
    11	Every module has three forms, written for the studio's engines:
    12	
    13	- **Still (Codex image tool, with a reference):** the default image step. The Codex tool has no negative field, so
    14	  every ban is written as an instruction ("do not …"). Paste the **reference opener** first, then the modules the shot
    15	  needs. The same text goes to Seedream 5 Pro, the paid alternative. Whether Seedream has a negative field is not
    16	  measured.
    17	- **Klein (FLUX.2-klein-4B, local, with a reference):** 2–3 short, concrete sentences. Long lists of bans do not help
    18	  a small model; a few physical facts do.
    19	- **Video (H3 Ref2VA / T2VA):** only on the motion modules (05, 06, 07, 08, 12, 13, 15). H3 at its default
    20	  `--cfg 1.0` ignores `--negative` (`~/tools/h3/run.py`), so the bans are written into the prompt here too. The
    21	  references carry identity only (`<Picture N>`); the prompt carries the whole scene. End every H3 prompt with the
    22	  **H3 UGC look line**.
    23	
    24	For a still-only module (01–04, 09–11, 14) in a text-only H3 take with no still, paste its Still form's physical
    25	sentences into the scene paragraph unchanged.
    26	
    27	**Reference opener** (paste before any Still form):
    28	
    29	```text
    30	Use the attached reference photo only for its framing, room, light and phone angle; do not copy the person's face, the content or any text in it. The person is the one in the attached cast photo, with exactly that face, hair and build. This is a real photo taken on a smartphone by a creator at home, not a studio photo and not an advertisement.
    31	```
    32	
    33	**H3 UGC look line** (the last paragraph of every H3 prompt):
    34	
    35	```text
    36	Photoreal live-action smartphone video, vertical 9:16, filmed by the person themselves on a modern phone: auto exposure and auto white balance, slight sensor noise in the shadows, the phone's own mild sharpening, small handheld micro-movement, imperfect framing. Real skin with visible pores and fine texture, real fabric, real room. It must read as a real person filming their own day, never as a studio advertisement: no studio lights, no smooth gimbal or dolly movement, no cinematic shallow focus, no beauty filter, no airbrushed skin, no text, no captions, no watermark. The face, hands and product stay exactly the same from the first frame to the last; no warping, no flicker.
    37	```
    38	
    39	## 01 — Natural skin and visible pores (UGC)
    40	
    41	Changes from the original: the 85 mm cinema close-up becomes an arm's-length front-camera close-up; the phone sees most
    42	of the face sharp, with the gentle wide-angle perspective of a lens held 40–60 cm away.
    43	
    44	**Still (Codex, with a reference):**
    45	
    46	```text
