ur steps, each a row with a named owner · accept: each of the four steps is a row with an owner and a gate, and the two steps that create a company or sign a client stop at him; nothing on this chain moves before the machine behind it stands · proves: `node scripts/governance/board-row-exists.mjs dxb-agency-chain` · status: planned · gate: his word · <!-- W14-L08 --> **(08) RIGHTS AND LICENSING — what the holding may legally SELL with each engine, model and music bed** — owner: the CEO decides the licences (a licence is a contract); the AUTHOR session keeps the record · finding: F067 · deliverable: a licence line for every engine, checkpoint and music source the studio touches — saying in one sentence what may be sold with it and what may not — plus the single OpenMontage AGPL question put to him once, in his language, for a yes or a no · accept: no engine, checkpoint or music source may be used on a paying client's job unless its licence line names a commercial right; the AGPL question carries his own answer or is visibly marked as waiting on him · proves: `bash scripts/b43/licence-line-gate.sh` · status: planned · gate: his word · <!-- W14-L09 --> **(09) THE MASTER ARCHIVE AND ITS BACKUP — one disk failure must not erase the films he said yes to** — owner: the AUTHOR session; the nightly backup service carries it · finding: F066 · deliverable: a code-named store holding every master film with its recipe and its seed beside it, inside the nightly backup, and a checksum for each master written into the job book — so a film can be proven whole, and brought back, after the workstation dies · accept: every master film has its checksum in the job book and a copy that is NOT on this workstation; a restore test takes one film out of the backup and brings it back whole and identical · proves: `bash scripts/backup/verify-master-archive.sh` · status: planned · gate: his word · <!-- W14-L10 --> **(10) THE CLIENT LOOP — a brief arrives, a revision round is counted, a delivery 