name: apple-design
description: >
  Cross-platform UI/UX design reviewer grounded in Apple's Human Interface Guidelines (123 pages
  pulled from developer.apple.com, including 57 component pages) plus a design-craft lens for
  distinctive, non-templated work. Use it to audit, review, critique, or improve any mobile app
  (iOS, Flutter, React Native) or desktop app (macOS, Tauri, Electron) design: design review, UI
  audit, HIG compliance, accessibility audit, dark mode, Liquid Glass or glassmorphism, navigation
  structure, iPhone Duo or foldable layouts, onboarding, forms, app icons, generative AI UX, or
  requests like "make this look less generic". Also use whenever the user shares screenshots,
  mockups, wireframes, Figma exports, or UI code and wants feedback, even if they only say "review
  my design" or "is this good UI". Works for SwiftUI, UIKit, AppKit, Flutter, React Native, Tauri,
  and Electron.
---
    76	Whatever he opens with — a greeting, a question, an order — **your first reply tells him where the
    77	work stands before anything else**, in four short lines in his language:
    78	
    79	1. what is finished and **waiting for his eye** (nothing is accepted until he looks — LAW B);
    80	2. what is **next**, and whether it waits on him or on you;
    81	3. what is **blocked on him** and cannot move without it;
    82	4. then answer what he actually said.
    83	
    84	**Empty is silent, not announced** (CEO order, 2026-09-22: *"gözünüzü bekleyen bir şey yok diye
    85	açılışta önüme gelmesin"*). Line 1 or line 3 is spoken only when it is true — something really is
    86	waiting for his eye, or really is blocked on him. When there is nothing there, that line is
    87	**dropped**, not replaced with "nothing is waiting" / "gözünüzü bekleyen bir şey yok". He reads
    88	four lines to find out what needs him; a line that exists only to say "nothing" costs him the read
    89	for zero content. This is scoped to this reply protocol only — it does not touch "zero is a real
    90	answer" above, which governs system state surfaces, not this greeting line.
    91	
    92	**The position is already in front of you** — the session-start hook carries where we left off,
    93	what is next and what waits on his approval; this file carries what the holding IS. **Never re-open a file
    94	to be told what you have already been told.** Measured 2026-08-10: a session re-read
    95	`MASTER_PLAN.md` §1 to answer "what is this project", and he saw it — *"ne diye tekrar tekrar
    96	okuyorsun"*. Open a file for what the injected text does NOT carry — a count, a row's detail, a
    97	live measurement — and then measure it rather than quote it.
    98	
    99	## 1. AUTHORITY — when two sources disagree
   100	
   101	1. The CEO's order in this conversation
   102	2. His most recent written directive — `docs/ceo-directives/`
   103	3. The spec that owns the contract — `HOLDING-OS-MASTER-PLAN/`
   104	4. The open work board
   105	5. `.planning/STATE.md` — the current state photo
   106	6. Code, tests and schemas — evidence of what **is**, never of what **should be**
   107	7. Anything older, any summary, any memory
   108	
   109	**Never silently choose between conflicting sources.** Name the conflict, apply this order,
   110	say what you did.
   111	
   112	His live sentence beats every written rule beneath it. A written rule that pulls outside what he asked
   113	for is not obeyed — it is reported to him in one line, and he decides.
   114	
   115	His word is intent, not a specification: every idea of his, written directives included, is tested
   116	for engineering soundness before it is agreed; a flaw, gap or better way is named unasked with
   117	its reason, and "right" is said only when it is. Then his word decides.
   118	<!-- CEO-OK: intent-not-spec-2026-10-19 -->
   224	  change; the research ruler and `tests/b46` when the research engine changes.
   225	
   226	## 6. Commit, tell, record
   227	
   228	- **Commit:** one per job, or one per independent piece; the message says what changed and why.
   229	- **The row:** a job that advances a board row rewrites that row in the same commit — what finished becomes
   230	  one dated note, `node scripts/board/note-done.mjs <ID> "<what finished>"`, never a story; what remains open
   231	  stays; the row closes only when all of it is finished (`dxb-close-row`)
   232	  <!-- CEO-OK: rows-keep-up-note-not-story-2026-10-09 -->. The commit's subject names the row in its scope —
   233	  `type(B51 …): …` — and the records ruler's R6 stops the STATE commit while a row is behind work so named. A row left behind by
   234	  another job's commit gets its note from that commit and its folder — measured, never guessed.
   235	- **Tell him** in his language, in a few lines, under three headings — *Sizi bekleyen · Değişen ·
   236	  Bulunan* <!-- CEO-OK: job-report-three-headings-2026-10-03 -->: what waits on him; what changed;
   237	  what was found outside the job, each with a recommendation. An empty heading is not written; a
   238	  defect fixed inside the job is not listed; the proof stays in the work until he asks.
   239	- **His words** are written once, in `scripts/governance/ceo-approvals.json`, and only where a record
   240	  must rest on his order or approval (LAW B) — never copied into commits, doors or STATE.
   241	- **STATE** is written once, when the job is told and nothing is left in hand (a session's end cannot
   242	  be seen coming): the three headings, a few lines each — what
   243	  finished and waits for his eye, what is next, what waits on him. No conversation, no quotes, no
   244	  hashes or counts beyond the one that names a job.
   245	- **The job folder** (`.planning/quick/<date>-<slug>/`) holds Sol's report and the raw evidence — only
   246	  for a job that went to Sol. Sol's raw dump (`*.raw`) and its brief (`*brief*.txt`, his words verbatim)
   247	  stay on the disk and out of git (`.gitignore`), and leave the disk after 90 days
   248	  (`scripts/governance/sol-dump-sweep.sh`, run after every commit)
   249	  <!-- CEO-OK: sol-dumps-swept-after-90-days-2026-10-06 -->; `SOL.md`, the verdict, is committed
   250	  <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-overall-look-fixes-2026-10-06 -->.
   251	
   252	## 7. Long commands, context and handover
   253	
   254	- **The 4-minute rules** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-cost-gate.py`): a command over 4 minutes is the
   255	  lead's; a subagent idle over 4 minutes is never resumed — its cache is the 5-minute one.
   256	- **The context gate** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-context-gate.py`) and **the clean break**: from
   257	  50 % used, hand over at the first clean break — the piece finished well, the tree committed, no
   258	  battery running, the report sent — even when reaching it takes the session to about 60 %; never in
   259	  the middle of a piece. The clean break and the quality decide, not the number; the lead judges it,
   260	  helpers included <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-the-clean-break-2026-10-06 -->. At 60 % the gate refuses
   261	  the Agent tool — a safety net, past which nothing new starts.
   262	- **The handover** is done by the engineer, never the CEO
   263	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-50-without-asking-2026-10-01 -->. A note in the scratchpad, in the author's
   264	  own voice, whose first line tells the successor to open this door — `Skill("dxb-team2")` — before
   265	  anything else: it carries the job on as its lead, the orchestrator, not as a fresh session
   266	  <!-- CEO-OK: successor-opens-dxb-team2-2026-10-09 -->; then the job's place in the whole, then his words of this job verbatim, every phase with
   267	  status and commit, the traps met, the first message the successor sends; session-only orders marked
   268	  as such; last, the path of this session's own conversation —
   269	  `~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl` — with the line *if
   270	  anything here is unclear, read the part you need there*

